<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Della Rovere</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of della Rovere</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The della Rovere were a family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> that rose through the Church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Francesco della Rovere</a> became Pope Sixtus IV (1471–1484) and his nephew Giuliano became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> (1503–1513). Through adoption and papal backing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-maria-i-della-rovere">Francesco Maria I della Rovere</a> became Duke of Urbino in 1508, and the family ruled Urbino until the death of Francesco Maria II in 1631.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/della-rovere.jpg" alt="Della Rovere" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZuppaDiCarlo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stemma_della_famiglia_Della_Rovere.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
